WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.560
This video explains VXLAN, the overlay that lets cloud virtual networks span many physical hosts.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.801
We cover the packet wrapping, the M T U math, and how the forwarding table learns.

00:00:13.001 --> 00:00:15.160
Think of mailing a letter through a courier.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.078
Your letter is the original Ethernet frame from a virtual machine.

00:00:19.478 --> 00:00:27.338
The courier puts it inside a larger envelope with a label saying which customer network it belongs to. That label is the V N I.

00:00:27.738 --> 00:00:33.253
The courier's truck only takes packages up to a fixed size. That size is the M T U.

00:00:33.653 --> 00:00:38.134
Because the envelope adds size, your letter must be smaller to still fit.

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:40.217
Four words.

00:00:40.617 --> 00:00:44.193
The V N I is a twenty four bit number naming the tenant network.

00:00:44.593 --> 00:00:50.073
A V TEP is the endpoint that wraps and unwraps frames, a host or a smart NIC.

00:00:50.473 --> 00:00:55.024
The M T U is the biggest packet a link carries, often fifteen hundred bytes.

00:00:55.424 --> 00:00:59.789
The F D B maps a MAC address to the remote V TEP that owns it.

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:02.394
Let's add up the cost.

00:01:02.794 --> 00:01:04.988
Fourteen bytes of outer Ethernet header.

00:01:05.388 --> 00:01:07.292
Twenty bytes of outer IPv4.

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:10.827
Eight bytes of UDP, on port forty seven eighty nine.

00:01:11.227 --> 00:01:30.232
Eight bytes of VXLAN header with the V N I. The outer Ethernet header doesn't count against the underlay's IP M T U, so the inner Ethernet header is charged instead, and the total is still fifty bytes: on a fifteen hundred byte underlay the inner IP M T U is fourteen fifty. Get this wrong and large packets silently vanish.

00:01:31.432 --> 00:01:31.955
Recap.

00:01:32.355 --> 00:01:35.780
VXLAN carries an Ethernet frame inside UDP.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.142
The V N I keeps tenants apart.

00:01:38.542 --> 00:01:42.710
Lower the inner M T U by fifty, or use jumbo frames underneath.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:48.195
The F D B learns which V TEP owns each MAC and forgets stale entries.

00:01:48.595 --> 00:01:51.486
Lesson thirty eight point two builds this in C.
